hen you're looking in  that it's so  linked and you can see people on  different political sides  defending their views or using sometimes  christianity in particular to say this  is god's will do you think that faith is  being weaponized in american politics in  a way that that is just unhealthy  i think that it has definitely been  weaponized in a way that's unhealthy  because i think that we've used faith as  a  way of  making people  other than us or that other than believe  wicked and therefore if you can make  them wicked and make them some kind of  of anti what god would want that it  justifies treating them unequally and  treating them unfairly which is why i  think people of faith have to say i'm  motivated by my faith but i want to  protect people's rights that may not  even have a faith i remember when i came  out for lgbtq rights many many years ago  and i even supported uh gay marriage  some of the clergymen that i preach for  are in their churches as a guest  minister said to me reverend al i don't  know if we could have you to church this  year because a lot of our parishioners  are not with you on that same-sex  marriage and i said well okay then i'm  not coming  i think when there is just a blurring of  the lines as there still seems to be in  america at times with faith and with  politics i mean  it can be that people do not see it as  the law they see it as imposing people's  views on them and i i wonder what you  think would come first an atheist  president of america or a female  president well uh uh i i don't i  probably would say a female president  uh but i don't rule it out i mean when i  got started as a as a youngster in the  movement i never thought i'd see a black  president so it's it it would not  shock me that either would come but my  guess would be a female president we  already now have a female vice president  indeed well it's it's still not happened  but i suppose whatever political side  you're on the candidate has to believe  in god and has to prove it in some way  which is striking regardless of how you  come at it just talking about the phrase  righteous troublemaker  and i was thinking about what happened  just over a year ago on the capitol  you know those  rioters  would probably describe themselves in a  similar way as righteous troublemakers  they believe in what they are doing they  believe in what they're marching for and  i suppose what would you say being  someone who's protested your whole life  and being so divided from those with a  view who have of what they're doing  being just as worthy as what you're  doing  i would say to them that what will make  the difference of whether what you're  doing is righteous or unrighteous is the  end goal  what are you trying to do they were  there that day january 6  to stop the certification of an election  that the american people had chosen a  president if you're going to have a  righteous cause the end the goal must be  righteous and your behavior to achieve  the goal must be righteous neither was  the case in the january 6 insurrection  because some say what happened on the  capitol was the beginning of something  not the end of something and 75 million  people voted for trump in the last  election and i i just wondered from your  side of things as it were  how do you see this playing out if the  sides just can't talk to each other  because you've you've built movements  i think that uh  i agree with those that say january 6  was the beginning  of something  they are not going anywhere they have  gone into states now changing voting  laws  or they have built an infrastructure  around this big lie that trump really  won  and that they want to in some way  control how votes are counted and how  votes are done  i think we're in for a long season  of confronting  voting rights and different sides  opposing different sides on these  questions and i don't know that we're  going to be able to get to this dialogue  of healing uh for a minute now i think  this is going to play itself out i think  the election  of joe biden and the four years of  donald trump has divided the united  states more than i've seen it in my  lifetime  looking at  movements then and looking at how things  can hopefully get better or improve of  course as i say you've been protesting  you've been on the streets for a long  long time and you've seen things that  you couldn't have imagined like a black  president like you just said but i was  just thinking about tactics and changing  approach of course many hailed the black  lives movement protests because they  didn't erupt in violence they didn't  erupt in riots although there have also  been divisions within that some factions  calling for the defunding of the police  just a word on that  where are you on that and where do you  think the movement is on that because  there is division i think as any  movement there's always going to be  disagreements within the movement uh you  know i grew up in the north uh the year  martin luther king was killed i was 13.  i was already youth director of of his  chapter in new york there were people  that disagreed with dr king they're  saying that they believed in  self-defense not non-violence they  believed in uh then you had those on the  other side that had condemned dr king uh  for coming out against the war in  vietnam saying that we should be for  whatever president johnson wanted  because he was a good civil rights  president so these disagreements exist i  think that we need to reform the police  but we need to keep a measure of  policing in our communities because we  have high crimes you've got as much a  problem with gun violence as you have a  problem of dealing with police that are  over the line and do things that are  wrong  and that in in many cases are criminal  uh so i think that it is restructuring  how you put the funds and how you fund  policing but you must have policing  three weeks after i did the eulogy for  george floyd's funeral i did the eulogy  of a one-year-old child  that was killed in a crossfire of gangs  fighting in brooklyn so we need both we  need good policing  funded properly that deal with things  like mental health and gun control and  there's also of course this push and  hope that the police themselves will  report bad cops will also come forward  and and have that culture change within  there as well as the training and the  policing that they've got to do that you  talk about and just thinking about  people who may be thinking of themselves  in the way that you've described a  righteous troublemaker talking about  making good trouble as you said right at  the beginning of our conversation and if  you think back to your your younger self  and how you've changed as a protester  and your tactics  what is your biggest regret and what's  the lesson you've learned what i've  learned is that you should not  shoot from the hill we often say  tit-for-tat report or somebody say  something i'm almost at at the beginning  of their last word answering them  be more thoughtful to make sure whatever  you say  and whatever you do and whatever tactics  you use is toward achieving the end goal  that you profess because people are  depending on you  and they expect you to be thoughtful not  just theatrical one of the controversies  that is still brought up by your critics  is your response to the 1991 crown  heights riots in new york  to remind our viewers these took place  after a seven-year-old black boy was  killed in a motorcade by a motorcade  transporting a rabbi violence broke out  after that death and a jewish student  was killed your critics say you never  called on the rioters to stop their  anti-semitic inspired violence is that  fair  that's totally erroneous because as the  state of new york did a study i was not  even in crown heights when the rabbi a  young rabbi student was killed i was  called by the family of the  seven-year-old kid the next day  but many in the fringe  right wing try and distort that i was  somehow should have called for them not  to do what they did that night when i  was living in new jersey but these are  the kinds of things that you have to  deal with and you keep going that is not  only unfair it's untrue that's why  that's why i wanted to ask you about it  i mean you did mark the 20th anniversary  of the incident you wrote an opinion  piece in the new york daily news and you  said our language and tone sometimes  exacerbated tensions and played to the  extremists and actually there was a  response from from the young man's  brother  um who responded saying he felt your  recollections were sanitized and and  distorted so there's obviously still a  lot of hurt and pain that but again  we're talking uh 30 what how many years  ago all you have to do is go to the  state report who by the way criticized  david dinkins the media and others but  said  sharpton was not there now i said that  when i came in  that maybe i could have as i did the  eulogy for the seven-year-old kid's  funeral said more things that would have  been uh that i they would say  differently but to say that i incited or  excused violence it's just not true and  even in his brother's statement saying  sanitize whatever as a man of opinion he  could not say that i incited violence i  i suppose it's just when you are looking  back at tactics and how protests happen  and the leadership i suppose that you've  had it's about also learning from that  which is what i wanted to ask you about  because you'll be well aware of these  criticisms over the years  right i think that what i've learned and  what i share  is keep your eye on the prize what is it  that you're going for don't react to the  crowd don't play to uh mrs martin luther  king coretta scott kings tell me don't  play to the cheap seats to get the  roaring of the crowd she said think  about what you're trying to achieve and  speak to that and that's what i've had  to learn i just wanted to ask about the  next generation of leaders that you have  in your site particularly around racial  equality are there some that you are  particularly keeping an eye on i have  many in my uh national action network  and some beyond that i'm looking at i  won't call names but there are some that  are very impressive and i tell them that  they've got to learn in their generation  what works and they're going to be  tested and you're going to be criticized  and being a troublemaker you're going to  have trouble because  the fact that you're questioning  speaking truth to power power is going  to answer you so make sure  you are absolutely committed and want to  be there to do that but keep your eye on  the prize don't make my mistakes  you can study people like me and others  but study also where we fell short and  correct them as we go and also health i  imagine you have an amazing regime  you could keep going for longer i  suppose you if you don't mind me  mentioning you've lost an enormous  amount of weight and you post about  drinking your kale juice every day and  you look great i've lost 176 pounds over  the last four years i am a vegetarian  eat one meal a day work out uh 35  minutes every morning and i'm in better  shape now at 67 than i was at 37. well  long long may it continue and do you  work out listening to james brown of  course a lot of times i do he was like  my dad first time i went to london james  brown brought me he played at the  hammersmith odium  and uh i worked with james brown he  brought me to london and i listened a  lot of his music is up tempo so it helps  me when i'm on the elliptical or lifting  weights that's what you're doing it too  uh reverend al sharpton thank you very  much indeed for your your time your  recollections and and some of your  lessons thank you god bless thank you so  much for being with us until we meet  again mask up stay safe and goodbye  [Music]  you
 

